{"kind":"artifact","text":"Exact-head implementation artifact at path ui for the decision-records web UI change, exact clean pushed head b60f3a406590c823bdac2ef51c70a6e21a467232 on branch request/decision-records-ui, cut from current main 6fad9e8a. Implementation report for planner request #10894 under my promise #10897.\n\nTHE WALK, WHICH IS THE FIRST CONDITION AND THE MOST USEFUL PART. Planner asked me to re-derive rather than transcribe, because the request was originally filed on the claim that this loop could not be done in the web UI at all. That premise IS stale — and a real gap remained. Both halves matter.\n\nI walked docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md step by step against the UI at current main, using the running resident and reading ui/src. I first confirmed the resident was serving the committed embed rather than a stale bundle: the page references assets/index-Clyj5ZUj.js, which is exactly the file committed under internal/service/uidist at main. I also took the kind definitions from the live resident's own vocabulary rather than from memory.\n\nStep by step:\n- Write the file and commit on a branch: OUT OF SCOPE, deliberately. No checkout in a browser, and the page says so itself.\n- Publish the artifact: MISSING. The Composer in Thread.tsx typed its replies as say, assert, promise, report, dissent or withdraw. No code path set any other kind, it built a body carrying only mentions, and it always sent exactly one basis — the thread it was replying into.\n- Propose adoption: MISSING, same cause. `propose` appeared in the toolbar only for reading existing proposals.\n- Ratify the proposal: PRESENT. The awaiting-ratification tab names the ratifier and the toolbar offers the act.\n- The review request, with body to, conditions, head and artifact, resting on BOTH the artifact and the proposal: MISSING, and structurally impossible — only one basis could ever be cited.\n- The promise: PRESENT. Ratify the verdict: PRESENT.\n- Sign the verdict with gs review, and merge: OUT OF SCOPE, deliberately, and this is the interesting exclusion. gs review signs only from a clean checkout sitting on the artifact's exact commit, and that binding is precisely what makes a verdict mean somebody read the code. A browser cannot supply it, and faking it would hollow out the review.\n- Follow the chain: PRESENT and good — path, commit, rests-on, rested-on-by, and every staleness flag, each reference a real resolving button.\n\nSo the gap was exactly THREE ACTS — publish an artifact, propose adoption, request review — with one structural cause underneath: every durable write in the browser was a reply into an open thread, carrying one basis and no body fields. The browser could not start a record, and could not fill a record's body. That is a much smaller and much sharper finding than \"the loop cannot be done\", and it is what the change addresses.\n\nOne more thing the walk established, which saved work: /v0/act already accepts any kind with any body and any rests_on. No service or fold change was needed. The gap was entirely in the browser.\n\nTHE CHANGE. Three things, none of which invent semantics.\n\nAdoption is looked up BY PATH, built once in buildRecordIndex over ratified, effective, unretired proposals. By path rather than by artifact deliberately: section 5 of the how-to publishes a second artifact at the same path and says the standing adoption already covers it. Keyed by artifact, every revision would read as unadopted and the UI would demand a redundant proposal.\n\nAn artifact row therefore offers exactly one next act — propose adoption when the path has none, request review when it has one, with the request citing the artifact AND the standing adoption as bases. Adoption before review is enforced by which button exists rather than by prose, which is the ordering the how-to insists on.\n\nThe composer can now carry a body and more than one basis. A request gets an addressee select populated from workroom.actors, so body.to is a resolved fingerprint rather than typed text, plus a conditions field; the send is blocked until both are present, because the fold refuses a request missing either. And a new publish dialog handles the one act that starts from nothing, offering the current thread's record as an opt-in basis for section 6's stamped predecessor.\n\nOne guard worth naming: after filing, the dialog waits for the projection to carry the new record before opening it. Without that, the page renders \"this thread is not in the projection\" about a record it just filed successfully — a false negative of exactly the kind this repository has been bitten by before.\n\nCONDITIONS MET, with gates I ran myself at this exact head:\n- make ui-check exit 0, and — the check that actually matters — the tree is still CLEAN after the rebuild, so the committed embed is reproducible from the source. Editing ui/ without rebuilding leaves the resident serving old assets while everything looks green; that trap is closed and demonstrated, not merely claimed.\n- go build ./... and go vet ./... OK; internal/service ok 26.466s, internal/ciguard ok 0.409s, internal/wireparity ok 0.523s.\n- npm test: 93 tests, 93 pass, 0 fail.\n\nMUTATION-VERIFIED, INCLUDING ONE THAT DID NOT FAIL. Thirteen mutations were run during implementation and each went red on a named test — dropping the ratified check, keying adoptions by artifact instead of path, dropping the adoption from the request's bases, always offering propose, dropping body.to and body.conditions, sending before the request is complete, truncating rests_on, removing the projection guard, ignoring the dialog's ticked basis, filing the wrong kind, and always citing a basis.\n\nBut one SURVIVED on the first pass and is the most valuable line in this report: filing the artifact with no body at all was not caught, because the test never inspected the POST payload. The test was strengthened to assert act, kind, text, body path and commit, and rests_on. I re-ran that exact mutation myself rather than accepting the fix on trust: with body set to undefined, \"a published artifact opens only once the projection carries it\" now fails. Reverted, tree clean.\n\nTHE STANDING UI REVIEW BAR, checked deliberately rather than assumed: no per-row projection scans (the artifact branch reads statement.body and one Map.get; the adoption map is built once alongside the existing maps); disclosure through real button elements, asserted in the test by tagName, with the dialog a real form with role dialog and aria-labels; references that resolve, because body.artifact is the artifact's own event id, body.head is copied from its body.commit, and body.to is a fingerprint from the roster — nothing typed by hand that could dangle; and handler-mutation sensitivity, which is where the surviving mutation was found.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and I would rather say it than let a reviewer assume otherwise. No durable event was filed from the browser during this work, so the new writes have not been exercised end-to-end against the real resident. The payloads are pinned by tests against the real components and the real api.act, and /v0/act is unchanged — but \"the fold accepts these exact records in the live log\" is unverified until somebody clicks it. That is worth one manual pass before merge, and it is the thing I would check first.\n\nAlso flagged, not fixed: a pre-existing directProposal branch in Toolbar.tsx does a per-basis find over projection.decisions and statements. I did not add it and did not change it, but it is the pattern the review bar rejects and it is already there.","body":{"branch":"request/decision-records-ui","commit":"b60f3a406590c823bdac2ef51c70a6e21a467232","conditions":"The decision-records loop is completable in the web UI for a person with the right roles: publish artifact, propose adoption, ratify, request review, promise, ratify verdict, follow the chain; gs review signing and gs merge are named deliberate exclusions because both bind to a clean checkout at an exact commit. make ui-check exit 0 with the tree clean after rebuild (committed embed reproducible); npm test 93/93; go build, go vet, internal/service, internal/ciguard, internal/wireparity all green at this exact head; thirteen mutations verified red plus one that initially survived, whose test was strengthened and the mutation re-run to failure.","head":"b60f3a406590c823bdac2ef51c70a6e21a467232","path":"ui"}}